---
source: crates/togi/tests/cli/mod.rs
expression: stdout(&ran)
---
Start or resume the night's tuning session in the foreground

Runs the per-CCD night policy: mapping, exact-profile qualification and requalification, and reproduction of suspect daily failures. If none is due, it can repeat tests at the current nonzero day profile. It requires a current stock attestation from `togi night` and never runs an all-zero trial. The night has no deadline: it continues across crash reboots and ends when you stop it or the tuner has no admissible work or hits a safety dead end. On resume, recorded defects can also stop the run. Needs root.

A session starts from a verified all-zero baseline. A clean stop restores and verifies CO 0. A BIOS change found on resume is recorded and the session continues. A journal from an older ruleset, schema or evidence epoch, and no newer one, is archived. A newer ruleset, schema or evidence epoch stops the run before another event is written; reset --all archives that session. Journal lines are colored on terminals and in the system journal unless the environment asks for no colour.

Every run requires an armed hardware watchdog before each nonzero profile application and each workload start. Manual startup checks once and refuses immediately; --tuning-boot waits up to 30 seconds. Loss detected at a later boundary stops new work. Cleanup attempts to restore and verify CO 0.

Only one day observer, run or reset can own this machine, even with different state directories. Stop the day service before tuning. The NixOS module creates /run/lock/togi.lock at boot; run can create it if it is missing. If the lock cannot be taken, no hardware is accessed and no event is written.

Prints one line per journal event and any final diagnostics to stderr, even on a terminal. Use `togi watch` in a separate terminal to see the dashboard.

--tuning-boot runs the unattended service with an armed hardware watchdog. The first durable journal append resets its consecutive restart-limit count and records a pending leave reason once. Dead ends and incompatible journals persist a short leave reason before clearing the saved GRUB entry.

Usage: togi run [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --config <path>
          JSON configuration file [default: /etc/togi/config.json]

      --state-dir <path>
          State directory
          
          [default: /var/lib/togi]

      --tuning-boot <file>
          Run as the tuning boot service: use the GRUB environment to persist a leave reason before restoring the day's boot selection, and reboot after a boot loop

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Examples:
  sudo togi run   Tune this machine tonight until you stop it or a dead end does
